-- constitution §41 `resolution.rule` — BARE IDENTITY + SEMANTIC DEMAND +
-- AVAILABLE HOMES -> ONE IDENTITY, OR A DIAGNOSTIC. gap[087].
--
--     law{ kind = invariant }      -- canonical
--     law{ kind = .invariant }     -- retirement debt
--
-- WHAT THIS FIXTURE EXISTS TO CATCH. §40 recorded obligation 1 satisfied
-- because both forms PARSE. Measured by value, that was the wrong test: the
-- dotted case compiled and the bare case died in the C backend on "use of
-- undeclared identifier 'eof'". The parser accepted the name and nothing bound
-- it. gap[087] correctly BLOCKED the §40/§41 retirement of 231 sites on this,
-- and recorded the durable rule: **a retirement obligation is not satisfied by
-- the replacement PARSING; it is satisfied by the replacement RESOLVING AND
-- LOWERING, proven by value.**
--
-- THE DEMAND COMES FROM THE OTHER OPERAND, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOT CIRCULAR.
-- §41's rule needs an expected descriptor known INDEPENDENTLY of the token
-- being resolved. In `k == eof` that is `k`, whose case-set was fixed by the
-- binding above it. Nothing about `eof` contributes to deciding what `eof`
-- means.
--
-- THE AMBIGUOUS SPELLING IS THE POINT, and it is why this fixture declares two
-- case-sets. `number` is a case of BOTH `token.kind` and `glyph.form`. A
-- resolver that indexed case names and picked the home that declared one would
-- answer whichever file parsed first — a wrong VALUE rather than a failed
-- build, which is the failure mode this repository keeps paying for. Here the
-- same spelling resolves to two DIFFERENT homes eight lines apart, and only the
-- demand tells them apart.
--
-- VERIFIED BY VALUE, and every wrong resolution prints a different number:
--
--   1     k == eof            resolves and is TRUE
--   10    eof == k            the reversed operand order also resolves
--   5     k != number         `number` takes token.kind, and k is not it
--   200   f == number         the SAME spelling takes glyph.form, and f IS it
--   30000 f == circle         must NOT fire — a real case of the right home
--   1000  k == number         must NOT fire — cross-home comparison is false
--
-- 216 is the only total. A case that resolved to nothing compares equal to
-- nothing and prints 0; one that resolved to a constant and compared equal to
-- everything prints 31216; a resolver that picked `number`'s home by index
-- rather than by demand prints 16 or 1216 depending on which it picked.
-- expect: 216
--
-- LOWERING IS NOT WORSE, which `law.canon.performance` (§42) makes a
-- requirement and not a nicety: the emitted C is `k == duo_token__kind_eof`,
-- byte-identical to what the dotted form produces, because the resolver
-- rewrites the bare name into THE SAME NODE the dotted and fully-named forms
-- already produce (A3 ONE EDGE — nothing downstream learns a bare name was
-- written).
--
-- WHAT IS NOT CLOSED. Descriptor CONSTRUCTION with a case field — `law{ kind =
-- invariant }` itself — still fails, in BOTH the bare and the dotted spelling,
-- and gap[087] separates it by name as an independent defect. This fixture
-- closes the COMPARISON half, which is the half the 231-site migration was
-- blocked on.

token: {
    kind: { name, number, eof }
    span: i64
}

glyph: {
    form: { circle, square, number }
    area: f64
}

main = (): i64
    n = 0
    k = token.kind.eof
    f = glyph.form.number

    if k == eof
        n = n + 1
    if eof == k
        n = n + 10
    if k != number
        n = n + 5

    -- Same spelling, other home. Decided by `f`, not by an index.
    if f == number
        n = n + 200

    -- Both of these resolve and are FALSE. A fixture that only asserted the
    -- true rows would pass on a resolver that answered "equal" to everything.
    if f == circle
        n = n + 30000
    if k == number
        n = n + 1000

    print("{n}")
    0
